ML_PROJECT_NOTEBOOKS["CML3113|ML_PROJECT"] = "{\"cells\": [{\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"> **DRAFT. The submission collector for this project is not set up yet. Do not hand this notebook out.**\\n\", \"\\n\", \"# CML3113 Machine Learning: Engineering ML project\\n\", \"**Part A: coded notebook (30 marks) and Part B: decision memo form (15 marks). Together 45 marks, scaled to 40 percent of the course. Online, 3 hours, individual work, marked automatically.**\\n\", \"**Window:** opens Monday 23 November 2026, 00:00 and closes Wednesday 25 November 2026, 23:59 Kampala time. Your own 3 hours start when you press Start on the entry page and cannot be paused; the window's close is the last cutoff, so start by Wednesday 25 November 2026, 20:59 to get the full 3 hours.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"This project uses only what you learned in Weeks 1 to 10 (up to Class 3). You work on real readings from a steel plant, `Steel_industry_data.csv` (DAEWOO Steel, Gwangyang, South Korea, 2018, one reading every 15 minutes), and answer two questions: **Question 1** how much is the energy the plant uses in a 15-minute reading (kWh) in months the model has never seen, and **Question 2** which readings should raise a warning. November and December are the test set and are opened **once**.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Data: Sathishkumar V E, Changsun Shin and Yongyun Cho (2021). Steel Industry Energy Consumption [Dataset]. UCI Machine Learning Repository. https://doi.org/10.24432/C52G8C. Licence CC BY 4.0.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## How to work\\n\", \"1. Fill in the identity cell below (in the issued notebook it is filled in for you; check it) and run it. Then run the supplied cell **Prepare the data and the libraries**. The data file is stored inside it, so you need no upload and no download.\\n\", \"2. Work from top to bottom. Replace each `TODO` and each `None`. Keep the function names, the argument order and the variable names exactly as printed. Run each cell and read what it prints. You may add your own cells. Your work is marked wherever it is, as long as the required function names exist when the notebook runs from the top.\\n\", \"3. Run the **self-check** cells to see which functions still need work. They are not marked.\\n\", \"4. Part B is a form (the `MEMO` cell). Nothing is written as an essay.\\n\", \"5. Choose **Runtime, then Restart session and run all**. Fix any error. Press **Ctrl+S** (File, then Save) now and then; the countdown reminds you 30 and 10 minutes before your deadline.\\n\", \"6. There is **no Submit button**. Your notebook saves itself to your Drive and a backup goes to your lecturer every 5 minutes. At your deadline your editing stops and your work is collected automatically. Use **Check my work** on the entry page to see whether your notebook runs from the top; press **I have finished** there if you finish early.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## Time plan (180 minutes)\\n\", \"| Step | Time |\\n\", \"|---|---|\\n\", \"| Setup: open your notebook, run the first cells | 10 min |\\n\", \"| Task 1: clean the rows, Chart 1 | 40 min |\\n\", \"| Task 2: split by month | 10 min |\\n\", \"| Task 3: the three models, MAE and RMSE, Chart 2 | 35 min |\\n\", \"| Task 4: the warning question | 15 min |\\n\", \"| Tasks 5 and 6: choose a model, open the test set once | 10 min |\\n\", \"| Task 7: the Part B memo form | 25 min |\\n\", \"| Check my notebook, restart and run all, submit | 25 min |\\n\", \"| Spare time (breaks, reconnecting) | 10 min |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## How it is marked\\n\", \"Everything is marked by the lecturer's computer. No person reads your notebook for marks. Your functions are run on **other rows that you cannot see** (hidden checks), and a few of your results on the file are compared with the correct values. Of the 30 Part A marks: 7 for cleaning the real data; 2 for two labelled charts; 2 for the split by month; 6 for the baseline, linear regression and decision tree; 3 for MAE and RMSE; 2 for no leakage (imputation and the baseline mean fitted on the training rows only); 3 for the warning question (probabilities, then the confusion matrix counts, precision and recall at 0.5); 2 for opening the test set once, after your choice, and its result; 3 for your results on the file, the same answer every time (keep `random_state=17`) and every required function and the MEMO form being there. Part B gives 15 marks for the memo form. A list of notebooks that look unusual is checked by the lecturer afterwards.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## Help during the window\\n\", \"Ask wording and technical questions on the class channel, 08:00 to 20:00 each day of the window (lecturer to confirm these hours). If something fails (Colab, the entry page, the form), report it at once on the class channel with a screenshot. The lecturer can authorise one fresh attempt, still ending at the window's close.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Rules (the same rules as Practical Test 1).** Individual work, online, from anywhere. **Permitted resources:** your lecture slides, your lab notebooks from Classes 1 to 3, the revision guide, the cheatsheet and your own course notes. Write the code yourself.\\n\", \"**No AI or outside help:** do not use Gemini, ChatGPT, Copilot or any other AI tool to generate, complete, rewrite, explain or debug your answers. Do not get answers from another person, collaborate or copy someone else's work.\\n\", \"**Understanding check:** after the window, any student may be asked, in an online call, to explain their submitted code or make a small change to it, without AI or another person's help. The copy checks and the identity checks run on every notebook.\\n\", \"**Declaration:** by submitting, I confirm that I completed this project myself without AI assistance or another person's help, using only the permitted resources. I can explain and change the code I submitted.\\n\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"FULL_NAME = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"EMAIL = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"STUDENT_ID = \\\"\\\"   # your Student Registration Number exactly as on your university record\\n\", \"COHORT = \\\"CML3113\\\"  # Do not edit: identifies your course for marking.\\n\", \"TEST_ID = \\\"ML_PROJECT\\\"  # Do not edit: identifies this assessment.\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Your data\\n\", \"Real readings from the electricity meters of a steel plant (DAEWOO Steel, Gwangyang, South Korea), one row every 15 minutes for the whole of 2018: 35,040 rows. Source: Sathishkumar V E, Changsun Shin and Yongyun Cho (2021). Steel Industry Energy Consumption [Dataset]. UCI Machine Learning Repository. https://doi.org/10.24432/C52G8C. Licence CC BY 4.0. The same file is used by every student.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"| Column | Meaning |\\n\", \"|---|---|\\n\", \"| date | Day and time of the reading, written DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM. Each day's last reading is written with that day's date and 00:00 (see Task 1). |\\n\", \"| Usage_kWh | Energy used in the 15 minutes, kWh. |\\n\", \"| Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh, Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh | Reactive power in the 15 minutes, kVarh. |\\n\", \"| CO2(tCO2) | Carbon dioxide, calculated from the energy used (it leaks the answer; Task 1 removes it). |\\n\", \"| Lagging_Current_Power_Factor, Leading_Current_Power_Factor | Power factors in percent, 0 to 100. Calculated from the energy and the reactive power, so they are not used as features. |\\n\", \"| NSM | Seconds since midnight (0 to 85500). |\\n\", \"| WeekStatus, Day_of_week | Weekday or Weekend, and the day's name. |\\n\", \"| Load_Type | Light_Load, Medium_Load or Maximum_Load: a time-of-day band. Not used. |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The target is `Usage_kWh` (kWh). The features are `FEATURES` = `Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh`, `Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh`, `NSM`, `weekend` (`weekend` is made in Task 1). A **record** is one row as a dictionary. A **list of records** is several rows. Your functions take and return these. Missing values are `None`. Calculate from the lists you are given. Do not use the global tables inside a function, because the marked checks give your functions other rows. The constants `TARGET`, `FEATURES`, `LIMIT` and the others from the setup cell may be used.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-support\"], \"cellView\": \"form\"}, \"source\": \"#@title Prepare the data and the libraries - Do not edit this cell\\n# IUEA-SETUP-CELL  (supplied: do not edit)\\n# Data: Steel Industry Energy Consumption, DAEWOO Steel, Gwangyang, South Korea, 2018, one reading every 15 minutes.\\n# Sathishkumar V E, Changsun Shin and Yongyun Cho (2021), UCI Machine Learning Repository, https://doi.org/10.24432/C52G8C. Licence CC BY 4.0.\\n# The file is stored inside this cell (compressed), so nothing is downloaded. It is written out and checked with SHA-256.\\nimport base64, bz2, hashlib, os\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport pandas as pd\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression, LogisticRegression\\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\\nfrom sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, precision_score, recall_score, roc_auc_score\\n\\nDATA_FILE = 'Steel_industry_data.csv'\\nDATA_SHA256 = '9b1cee6f9cb9cd9df2b95814ca90a9a2ff15b7f5f1fba0fae3c643e82072eacc'\\n_PACKED = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n__IUEA_PACKED_DATA__\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ndef _data_is_intact():\\n    return os.path.exists(DATA_FILE) and hashlib.sha256(open(DATA_FILE, \\\"rb\\\").read()).hexdigest() == DATA_SHA256\\nif not _data_is_intact():\\n    _raw = bz2.decompress(base64.b64decode(\\\"\\\".join(_PACKED.split())))\\n    if hashlib.sha256(_raw).hexdigest() != DATA_SHA256:\\n        raise RuntimeError(\\\"The data stored in this cell is damaged. Do not edit this cell. Tell your lecturer.\\\")\\n    with open(DATA_FILE, \\\"wb\\\") as _f:\\n        _f.write(_raw)\\n\\nTARGET = 'Usage_kWh'          # Question 1: the energy the plant uses in a 15-minute reading\\nTARGET_UNIT = 'kWh'\\nFEATURES = ['Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh', 'Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh', 'NSM', 'weekend']\\n# the power factors are left out: they are calculated from Usage_kWh and the reactive power, so they would leak the answer; CO2(tCO2) is removed in Task 1 for the same reason\\nLIMIT = 60.0          # a reading is a WARNING (1) when its target is at or above this limit, in kWh (invented teaching number)\\nMAX_MAE = 10.0         # the client accepts a typical prediction error (MAE) of at most this many kWh (invented teaching number)\\nMAX_MS = 50               # and a prediction time of at most this many milliseconds (invented teaching number)\\nLATENCY_MS = {'baseline': 1, 'linear': 3, 'tree': 30}   # invented teaching numbers\\nTHRESHOLD = 0.5       # the decision threshold: a warning when the probability is at or above 0.5 (fixed for the whole project)\\n\\ndef records_from_table(table):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"A list of dictionaries, one per row. Missing values (NaN) become None.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    objects = table.astype(object)\\n    return objects.where(table.notna(), None).to_dict(\\\"records\\\")\\n\\nraw_table = pd.read_csv(DATA_FILE, encoding=\\\"utf-8-sig\\\")    # the file starts with a byte-order mark: utf-8-sig removes it\\nall_records = records_from_table(raw_table)\\ndevelopment_records = [r for r in all_records if int(r[\\\"date\\\"][3:5]) <= 10]   # January to October. November and December are the TEST SET.\\nprint(\\\"Rows in the file:\\\", len(raw_table))\\nprint(\\\"Rows you may develop with (January to October):\\\", len(development_records))\\nprint(raw_table.head())\\n\\n# Automatic backup (supplied). Inside Colab, a copy of the notebook on your screen goes to your lecturer every 5 minutes.\\n# You do not need to do anything. Your work is collected automatically at your deadline.\\ndef _iuea_backup_start(address='https://iuea-test-entry.jothammartin-wambi.workers.dev/api/backup', minutes=5):\\n    import json, threading, time, urllib.request\\n    try:\\n        from IPython import get_ipython\\n        shell = get_ipython()\\n        if shell is None or \\\"google.colab\\\" not in type(shell).__module__:\\n            return                                   # not in Colab (the marking computer, Check my notebook): no backup\\n        from google.colab import _message, output\\n        from IPython.display import display, HTML\\n    except Exception:\\n        return\\n    attempt = str(globals().get(\\\"_IUEA_ALLOCATION\\\", \\\"\\\"))\\n    if not attempt:\\n        print(\\\"Backup is off: run the first cell (your details and the countdown) first, then this cell again.\\\")\\n        return\\n    old = globals().get(\\\"_iuea_backup_thread\\\")\\n    if old is not None and old.is_alive():\\n        return\\n    note = display(HTML(\\\"\\\"), display_id=True)\\n    packed = _PACKED.strip()\\n    def send():\\n        notebook = _message.blocking_request(\\\"get_ipynb\\\", timeout_sec=60)[\\\"ipynb\\\"]\\n        for cell in notebook.get(\\\"cells\\\", []):\\n            text = \\\"\\\".join(cell.get(\\\"source\\\", \\\"\\\")) if isinstance(cell.get(\\\"source\\\"), list) else str(cell.get(\\\"source\\\", \\\"\\\"))\\n            if \\\"# IUEA-SETUP-CELL\\\" in text:\\n                cell[\\\"source\\\"] = text.replace(packed, \\\"__IUEA_\\\" + \\\"PACKED_DATA__\\\")\\n        href = str(output.eval_js(\\\"location.href\\\"))[:500]\\n        body = json.dumps({\\\"attempt\\\": attempt, \\\"href\\\": href, \\\"notebook\\\": json.dumps(notebook)}).encode(\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n        request = urllib.request.Request(address, data=body, headers={\\\"Content-Type\\\": \\\"application/json\\\"}, method=\\\"POST\\\")\\n        with url