inus the validation MAE of your recommended model, in kWh (0 if you recommend the baseline). | 1 |\\n\", \"| `limits` | The letters of the **three** statements below that are **true limits of this study**. Three different letters. One mark for each true one. | 3 |\\n\", \"| `next_step` | One letter: the best next step. | 1 |\\n\", \"| `answer_choice`, `answer_test_set` | Two short written answers of 8 to 80 words each. One mark each. | 2 |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Limits.** Choose the three statements that are TRUE and are limits of this study (some statements are false):\\n\", \"- **A.** The test set is only November and December of the same year, so the test metrics are one estimate that may be luckier or unluckier than the truth.\\n\", \"- **B.** The model with the lowest training error is always the best model for new months.\\n\", \"- **C.** The client limits (the error limit and the time limit) and the warning limit are invented teaching numbers; a real client would set their own.\\n\", \"- **D.** The baseline model should be left out of the comparison because it uses no features.\\n\", \"- **E.** Because the test set was opened only once, the test metrics have no uncertainty at all.\\n\", \"- **F.** All the readings come from one steel plant in one year (2018), so the model may not hold for another plant or for later years.\\n\", \"- **G.** Fitting the imputer on the training set only makes the validation results unreliable.\\n\", \"- **H.** The validation set is only two months (September and October), so the validation errors could change a lot with two other months.\\n\", \"- **I.** The test MAE proves that the model will be this accurate in every month of every year.\\n\", \"- **J.** RMSE is always smaller than MAE, so MAE is the more severe measure and should be the only one reported.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Next step.** Choose the one best next step:\\n\", \"- **A.** Collect readings from more years (or another plant) and check the chosen model again on months it 