ML_PROJECT_NOTEBOOKS["CML3111|ML_REHEARSAL"] = "{\"cells\": [{\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"# CML3111 Machine Learning: Engineering ML project, REHEARSAL (unmarked)\\n\", \"**This is a rehearsal. Nothing here is marked or submitted.** It has the same structure, the same task wording and the same kinds of check as the marked project, on the same steel plant file, but with a different target: here you predict **the lagging reactive power the plant draws in a 15-minute reading** (kVarh), with its own warning limit. The marked project predicts the energy used (kWh). Your numbers here will not be the numbers you get in the project. Released Friday 20 November 2026, right after Class 3. The project window opens Monday 23 November 2026, 00:00 and closes Wednesday 25 November 2026, 23:59 Kampala time.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**How to use it.** Open it from the rehearsal entry link, exactly as you will open the project: type your registration number, confirm your name, press Start and click Open my project in Colab. It is your own copy in Drive and it saves itself. Work through it as if it were the real project, with a 3-hour timer if you can. Use the self-check cells, the Check my work button on the entry page, and I have finished when you are done. In the rehearsal nothing is locked and nothing is marked. A solved walk-through is not given: the point is that you can do it yourself.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"This project uses only what you learned in Weeks 1 to 10 (up to Class 3). You work on real readings from a steel plant, `Steel_industry_data.csv` (DAEWOO Steel, Gwangyang, South Korea, 2018, one reading every 15 minutes), and answer two questions: **Question 1** how much is the lagging reactive power the plant draws in a 15-minute reading (kVarh) in months the model has never seen, and **Question 2** which readings should raise a warning. November and December are the test set and are opened **once**.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Data: Sathishkumar V E, Changsun Shin and Yongyun Cho (2021). Steel Industry Energy Consumption [Dataset]. UCI Machine Learning Repository. https://doi.org/10.24432/C52G8C. Licence CC BY 4.0.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## How to work\\n\", \"1. Fill in the identity cell below (in the issued notebook it is filled in for you; check it) and run it. Then run the supplied cell **Prepare the data and the libraries**. The data file is stored inside it, so you need no upload and no download.\\n\", \"2. Work from top to bottom. Replace each `TODO` and each `None`. Keep the function names, the argument order and the variable names exactly as printed. Run each cell and read what it prints. You may add your own cells. Your work is marked wherever it is, as long as the required function names exist when the notebook runs from the top.\\n\", \"3. Run the **self-check** cells to see which functions still need work. They are not marked.\\n\", \"4. Part B is a form (the `MEMO` cell). Nothing is written as an essay.\\n\", \"5. Choose **Runtime, then Restart session and run all**. Fix any error. Press **Ctrl+S** (File, then Save) now and then; the countdown reminds you 30 and 10 minutes before your deadline.\\n\", \"6. There is **no Submit button**. Your notebook saves itself to your Drive and a backup goes to your lecturer every 5 minutes. At your deadline your editing stops and your work is collected automatically. Use **Check my work** on the entry page to see whether your notebook runs from the top; press **I have finished** there if you finish early.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## Time plan (180 minutes)\\n\", \"| Step | Time |\\n\", \"|---|---|\\n\", \"| Setup: open your notebook, run the first cells | 10 min |\\n\", \"| Task 1: clean the rows, Chart 1 | 40 min |\\n\", \"| Task 2: split by month | 10 min |\\n\", \"| Task 3: the three models, MAE and RMSE, Chart 2 | 35 min |\\n\", \"| Task 4: the warning question | 15 min |\\n\", \"| Tasks 5 and 6: choose a model, open the test set once | 10 min |\\n\", \"| Task 7: the Part B memo form | 25 min |\\n\", \"| Check my notebook, restart and run all, submit | 25 min |\\n\", \"| Spare time (breaks, reconnecting) | 10 min |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## How it is marked\\n\", \"Everything is marked by the lecturer's computer. No person reads your notebook for marks. Your functions are run on **other rows that you cannot see** (hidden checks), and a few of your results on the file are compared with the correct values. Of the 30 Part A marks: 7 for cleaning the real data; 2 for two labelled charts; 2 for the split by month; 6 for the baseline, linear regression and decision tree; 3 for MAE and RMSE; 2 for no leakage (imputation and the baseline mean fitted on the training rows only); 3 for the warning question (probabilities, then the confusion matrix counts, precision and recall at 0.5); 2 for opening the test set once, after your choice, and its result; 3 for your results on the file, the same answer every time (keep `random_state=17`) and every required function and the MEMO form being there. Part B gives 15 marks for the memo form. A list of notebooks that look unusual is checked by the lecturer afterwards.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"## Help during the window\\n\", \"Ask wording and technical questions on the class channel, 08:00 to 20:00 each day of the window (lecturer to confirm these hours). If something fails (Colab, the entry page, the form), report it at once on the class channel with a screenshot. The lecturer can authorise one fresh attempt, still ending at the window's close.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Rules (the same rules as Practical Test 1).** Individual work, online, from anywhere. **Permitted resources:** your lecture slides, your lab notebooks from Classes 1 to 3, the revision guide, the cheatsheet and your own course notes. Write the code yourself.\\n\", \"**No AI or outside help:** do not use Gemini, ChatGPT, Copilot or any other AI tool to generate, complete, rewrite, explain or debug your answers. Do not get answers from another person, collaborate or copy someone else's work.\\n\", \"**Understanding check:** after the window, any student may be asked, in an online call, to explain their submitted code or make a small change to it, without AI or another person's help. The copy checks and the identity checks run on every notebook.\\n\", \"**Declaration:** by submitting, I confirm that I completed this project myself without AI assistance or another person's help, using only the permitted resources. I can explain and change the code I submitted.\\n\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"FULL_NAME = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"EMAIL = \\\"\\\"\\n\", \"STUDENT_ID = \\\"\\\"   # your Student Registration Number exactly as on your university record\\n\", \"COHORT = \\\"CML3111\\\"  # Do not edit: identifies your course for marking.\\n\", \"TEST_ID = \\\"ML_PROJECT_REHEARSAL\\\"  # Do not edit: identifies this assessment.\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Your data\\n\", \"Real readings from the electricity meters of a steel plant (DAEWOO Steel, Gwangyang, South Korea), one row every 15 minutes for the whole of 2018: 35,040 rows. Source: Sathishkumar V E, Changsun Shin and Yongyun Cho (2021). Steel Industry Energy Consumption [Dataset]. UCI Machine Learning Repository. https://doi.org/10.24432/C52G8C. Licence CC BY 4.0. The same file is used by every student.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"| Column | Meaning |\\n\", \"|---|---|\\n\", \"| date | Day and time of the reading, written DD/MM/YYYY HH:MM. Each day's last reading is written with that day's date and 00:00 (see Task 1). |\\n\", \"| Usage_kWh | Energy used in the 15 minutes, kWh. |\\n\", \"| Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh, Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh | Reactive power in the 15 minutes, kVarh. |\\n\", \"| CO2(tCO2) | Carbon dioxide, calculated from the energy used (it leaks the answer; Task 1 removes it). |\\n\", \"| Lagging_Current_Power_Factor, Leading_Current_Power_Factor | Power factors in percent, 0 to 100. Calculated from the energy and the reactive power, so they are not used as features. |\\n\", \"| NSM | Seconds since midnight (0 to 85500). |\\n\", \"| WeekStatus, Day_of_week | Weekday or Weekend, and the day's name. |\\n\", \"| Load_Type | Light_Load, Medium_Load or Maximum_Load: a time-of-day band. Not used. |\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The target is `Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh` (kVarh). The features are `FEATURES` = `Usage_kWh`, `Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh`, `NSM`, `weekend` (`weekend` is made in Task 1). A **record** is one row as a dictionary. A **list of records** is several rows. Your functions take and return these. Missing values are `None`. Calculate from the lists you are given. Do not use the global tables inside a function, because the marked checks give your functions other rows. The constants `TARGET`, `FEATURES`, `LIMIT` and the others from the setup cell may be used.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-support\"], \"cellView\": \"form\"}, \"source\": \"#@title Prepare the data and the libraries - Do not edit this cell\\n# IUEA-SETUP-CELL  (supplied: do not edit)\\n# Data: Steel Industry Energy Consumption, DAEWOO Steel, Gwangyang, South Korea, 2018, one reading every 15 minutes.\\n# Sathishkumar V E, Changsun Shin and Yongyun Cho (2021), UCI Machine Learning Repository, https://doi.org/10.24432/C52G8C. Licence CC BY 4.0.\\n# The file is stored inside this cell (compressed), so nothing is downloaded. It is written out and checked with SHA-256.\\nimport base64, bz2, hashlib, os\\nimport numpy as np\\nimport pandas as pd\\nimport matplotlib.pyplot as plt\\nfrom datetime import datetime, timedelta\\nfrom sklearn.pipeline import make_pipeline\\nfrom sklearn.impute import SimpleImputer\\nfrom sklearn.preprocessing import StandardScaler\\nfrom sklearn.dummy import DummyRegressor\\nfrom sklearn.linear_model import LinearRegression, LogisticRegression\\nfrom sklearn.tree import DecisionTreeRegressor\\nfrom sklearn.metrics import confusion_matrix, precision_score, recall_score, roc_auc_score\\n\\nDATA_FILE = 'Steel_industry_data.csv'\\nDATA_SHA256 = '9b1cee6f9cb9cd9df2b95814ca90a9a2ff15b7f5f1fba0fae3c643e82072eacc'\\n_PACKED = \\\"\\\"\\\"\\n__IUEA_PACKED_DATA__\\n\\\"\\\"\\\"\\ndef _data_is_intact():\\n    return os.path.exists(DATA_FILE) and hashlib.sha256(open(DATA_FILE, \\\"rb\\\").read()).hexdigest() == DATA_SHA256\\nif not _data_is_intact():\\n    _raw = bz2.decompress(base64.b64decode(\\\"\\\".join(_PACKED.split())))\\n    if hashlib.sha256(_raw).hexdigest() != DATA_SHA256:\\n        raise RuntimeError(\\\"The data stored in this cell is damaged. Do not edit this cell. Tell your lecturer.\\\")\\n    with open(DATA_FILE, \\\"wb\\\") as _f:\\n        _f.write(_raw)\\n\\nTARGET = 'Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh'          # Question 1: the lagging reactive power the plant draws in a 15-minute reading\\nTARGET_UNIT = 'kVarh'\\nFEATURES = ['Usage_kWh', 'Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh', 'NSM', 'weekend']\\n# the power factors are left out: they are calculated from the energy and the reactive power, so they would leak the answer; CO2(tCO2) is removed in Task 1 for the same reason\\nLIMIT = 40.0          # a reading is a WARNING (1) when its target is at or above this limit, in kVarh (invented teaching number)\\nMAX_MAE = 6.0         # the client accepts a typical prediction error (MAE) of at most this many kVarh (invented teaching number)\\nMAX_MS = 50               # and a prediction time of at most this many milliseconds (invented teaching number)\\nLATENCY_MS = {'baseline': 1, 'linear': 3, 'tree': 30}   # invented teaching numbers\\nTHRESHOLD = 0.5       # the decision threshold: a warning when the probability is at or above 0.5 (fixed for the whole project)\\n\\ndef records_from_table(table):\\n    \\\"\\\"\\\"A list of dictionaries, one per row. Missing values (NaN) become None.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n    objects = table.astype(object)\\n    return objects.where(table.notna(), None).to_dict(\\\"records\\\")\\n\\nraw_table = pd.read_csv(DATA_FILE, encoding=\\\"utf-8-sig\\\")    # the file starts with a byte-order mark: utf-8-sig removes it\\nall_records = records_from_table(raw_table)\\ndevelopment_records = [r for r in all_records if int(r[\\\"date\\\"][3:5]) <= 10]   # January to October. November and December are the TEST SET.\\nprint(\\\"Rows in the file:\\\", len(raw_table))\\nprint(\\\"Rows you may develop with (January to October):\\\", len(development_records))\\nprint(raw_table.head())\\n\\n# Automatic backup (supplied). Inside Colab, a copy of the notebook on your screen goes to your lecturer every 5 minutes.\\n# You do not need to do anything. Your work is collected automatically at your deadline.\\ndef _iuea_backup_start(address='https://iuea-test-entry.jothammartin-wambi.workers.dev/api/backup', minutes=5):\\n    import json, threading, time, urllib.request\\n    try:\\n        from IPython import get_ipython\\n        shell = get_ipython()\\n        if shell is None or \\\"google.colab\\\" not in type(shell).__module__:\\n            return                                   # not in Colab (the marking computer, Check my notebook): no backup\\n        from google.colab import _message, output\\n        from IPython.display import display, HTML\\n    except Exception:\\n        return\\n    attempt = str(globals().get(\\\"_IUEA_ALLOCATION\\\", \\\"\\\"))\\n    if not attempt:\\n        print(\\\"Backup is off: run the first cell (your details and the countdown) first, then this cell again.\\\")\\n        return\\n    old = globals().get(\\\"_iuea_backup_thread\\\")\\n    if old is not None and old.is_alive():\\n        return\\n    note = display(HTML(\\\"\\\"), display_id=True)\\n    packed = _PACKED.strip()\\n    def send():\\n        notebook = _message.blocking_request(\\\"get_ipynb\\\", timeout_sec=60)[\\\"ipynb\\\"]\\n        for cell in notebook.get(\\\"cells\\\", []):\\n            text = \\\"\\\".join(cell.get(\\\"source\\\", \\\"\\\")) if isinstance(cell.get(\\\"source\\\"), list) else str(cell.get(\\\"source\\\", \\\"\\\"))\\n            if \\\"# IUEA-SETUP-CELL\\\" in text:\\n                cell[\\\"source\\\"] = text.replace(packed, \\\"__IUEA_\\\" + \\\"PACKED_DATA__\\\")\\n        href = str(output.eval_js(\\\"location.href\\\"))[:500]\\n        body = json.dumps({\\\"attempt\\\": attempt, \\\"href\\\": href, \\\"notebook\\\": json.dumps(notebook)}).encode(\\\"utf-8\\\")\\n        request = urllib.request.Request(address, data=body, headers={\\\"Content-Type\\\": \\\"application/json\\\"}, method=\\\"POST\\\")\\n        with urllib.request.urlopen(request, timeout=60) as reply:\\n            return json.loads(reply.read().decode(\\\"utf-8\\\"))\\n    def loop():\\n        while True:\\n            try:\\n                answer = send() or {}\\n                result = answer.get(\\\"result\\\") or {}\\n                if result.get(\\\"other_file\\\"):\\n                    note.update(HTML('<div style=\\\"border:3px solid #b3261e;background:#fff0ee;padding:10px;font-size:16px;color:#7a1712\\\">'\\n                                     '<b>You are working in a different file from your issued notebook.</b> Your work here is still backed up. '\\n                                     'Go back to the entry page and click Resume, or keep working here and tell your lecturer on the class channel.</div>'))\\n                elif answer.get(\\\"ok\\\"):\\n                    note.update(HTML('<div style=\\\"color:#526577;font-size:13px\\\">Automatic backup sent at ' + time.strftime(\\\"%H:%M\\\") + '. Your notebook also saves itself; press Ctrl+S now and then.</div>'))\\n                elif result.get(\\\"closed\\\") or \\\"deadline\\\" in str(answer.get(\\\"error\\\", \\\"\\\")):\\n                    note.update(HTML('<div style=\\\"color:#526577;font-size:13px\\\">Your time has ended. Your work was collected at your deadline.</div>'))\\n                    return\\n            except Exception:\\n                pass\\n            time.sleep(minutes * 60)\\n    thread = threading.Thread(target=loop, name=\\\"iuea-backup\\\", daemon=True)\\n    thread.start()\\n    globals()[\\\"_iuea_backup_thread\\\"] = thread\\n_iuea_backup_start()\\n\", \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 1: Clean the rows and look at them (7 automatic marks, Weeks 3 to 7)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"A **record** is one row written as a dictionary, with the column names of the file as keys. A missing value is `None`.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"What is really in this file (checked before the project): no missing cells and no repeated rows, but three things you must handle.\\n\", \"- Each day's **last** reading is written with that day's date and the time `00:00` (for example `01/01/2018 00:00` comes straight after `01/01/2018 23:45`). Its real time is midnight at the **end** of that day, so one day must be added to its timestamp.\\n\", \"- One reading (7 November) has every measurement 0: the meter recorded nothing.\\n\", \"- `CO2(tCO2)` is calculated from `Usage_kWh`, so keeping it would leak the answer (data leakage).\\n\", \"\\n\", \"`clean_rows(records)` returns a cleaned **copy** of the records, in the same order. For each record:\\n\", \"- make `timestamp`, a text `\\\"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM\\\"` from `date` (format `\\\"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\\\"`), with one day added when the time is 00:00; make `month`, the month **as written** in `date` (1 to 12); make `weekend`, `1` when `WeekStatus` is `\\\"Weekend\\\"`, else `0`;\\n\", \"- if the timestamp was already seen, skip the row (keep only the first);\\n\", \"- drop the row if `TARGET` is `None`, or if `Usage_kWh` and both power factors are all 0 (nothing recorded), or if a value is impossible: `Usage_kWh` or either reactive power below 0, a power factor below 0 or above 100 (0 and 100 are valid), `NSM` below 0 or above 85500;\\n\", \"- remove the key `CO2(tCO2)`. Do not fill a missing value: it stays `None`, and the models do the imputation later from the training set only.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The marking checks give your function other rows that contain these problems and others, so write every rule even if this file does not need it. Never change the list you were given. Then use your function on the development rows and draw **Chart 1**: `Usage_kWh` across, `Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh` up, with a title, both axis names and the unit (kVarh).\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-clean\"]}, \"source\": [\"def clean_rows(records):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return a cleaned copy of the records (see the task text above).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    # TODO 1: make an empty list called kept and an empty set called seen.\\n\", \"    # TODO 2: loop over records. when = datetime.strptime(record[\\\"date\\\"], \\\"%d/%m/%Y %H:%M\\\"); month = when.month\\n\", \"    #         if when.hour == 0 and when.minute == 0: when = when + timedelta(days=1)\\n\", \"    #         timestamp = when.strftime(\\\"%Y-%m-%d %H:%M\\\"). If timestamp is in seen, skip the row (continue); otherwise add it to seen.\\n\", \"    # TODO 3: skip the row if record[TARGET] is None, if nothing was recorded, or if a value is impossible (see the task text).\\n\", \"    # TODO 4: row = a copy of record without \\\"CO2(tCO2)\\\"; add row[\\\"timestamp\\\"], row[\\\"month\\\"] and row[\\\"weekend\\\"]; add row to kept.\\n\", \"    return None   # TODO 5: return kept instead\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-clean\"]}, \"source\": [\"development_rows = None      # TODO: call clean_rows on development_records\\n\", \"print(\\\"Rows kept:\\\", None)    # TODO: replace None with the number of rows in development_rows\\n\", \"\\n\", \"figure, axes = plt.subplots()\\n\", \"# TODO: axes.scatter(...): FEATURES[0] on the x axis, TARGET on the y axis, small points (s=2) (hint: table = pd.DataFrame(development_rows))\\n\", \"# TODO: axes.set_title(...), axes.set_xlabel(...) and axes.set_ylabel(...). Write the unit in the y label.\\n\", \"chart_1 = figure\\n\", \"plt.show()\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Self-study note: why split by month, and why a random split leaks\\n\", \"Readings 15 minutes apart are almost the same. If you shuffle the rows and split at random, the validation set is full of near-copies of training rows, and the model also learns from days that come after the ones it is checked on. The score then looks better than it will be next month. That is **leakage**: information the model would not have when it is really used reaches it during training.\\n\", \"Splitting by month keeps whole months apart and keeps the future in the future: train on January to August, check on September and October, and keep November and December closed for the final test.\\n\", \"Slides: Class 3, \\\"Train-test contamination: validation rows in the training set make the score look perfect\\\" and \\\"We choose max_depth on the validation set and keep the test set sealed\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 2: Split by month (2 automatic marks, Week 10)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"`time_split(records)` receives cleaned records and returns a dictionary with three lists of `timestamp` values, each in the order the records came in:\\n\", \"- `\\\"train\\\"`: months 1 to 8, January to August (the **training set**);\\n\", \"- `\\\"validation\\\"`: months 9 and 10, September and October (the **validation set**);\\n\", \"- `\\\"test\\\"`: months 11 and 12, November and December (the **test set**).\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Why not a random split?** Readings 15 minutes apart are almost the same. A random split puts near-copies of the same hour in the training set and the validation set, and lets the model learn from days that come after the ones it is tested on. The score would look better than it will be on next month. Splitting by month keeps whole months apart and keeps the future in the future. Your development rows have no November or December rows, so their `\\\"test\\\"` list is empty. That is correct.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"When you pick the training and validation rows, look the timestamps up in a **set**. Looking each one up in a list of 23,000 timestamps makes the cell run for minutes, and the marking computer stops a cell after 30 seconds.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-split\"]}, \"source\": [\"def time_split(records):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return {\\\"train\\\": [...], \\\"validation\\\": [...], \\\"test\\\": [...]} (lists of timestamp, in input order).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    # TODO: for each record, look at record[\\\"month\\\"] and put record[\\\"timestamp\\\"] in the right list.\\n\", \"    return None\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-split\"]}, \"source\": [\"split_ids = None     # TODO: time_split(development_rows)\\n\", \"train_ids, valid_ids = None, None   # TODO: set(split_ids[\\\"train\\\"]) and set(split_ids[\\\"validation\\\"]) (a set makes the look-up fast)\\n\", \"train_rows = None    # TODO: the development rows whose timestamp is in train_ids\\n\", \"valid_rows = None    # TODO: the development rows whose timestamp is in valid_ids\\n\", \"print(\\\"Training rows:\\\", None, \\\"| validation rows:\\\", None)   # TODO: replace the Nones with the two counts\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Self-study note: fit on the training rows only (leakage)\\n\", \"Every step that learns a number from data (the median used to fill a missing value, the mean of the baseline model, the mean and standard deviation of a scaler) must learn it from the **training rows only**, then apply the same numbers to the validation rows. A pipeline does this for you: `make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\"), model)` learns the medians inside `fit` and only reuses them inside `predict`.\\n\", \"If the medians or the mean came from all the rows, the model would have seen the rows it is judged on, and the error would look smaller than it really is.\\n\", \"Slides: Class 3, \\\"Data leakage is information the model would not have at prediction time reaching it during training\\\" and \\\"The pipeline rule: fit on the training set only, then apply the same numbers\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 3: Question 1: a baseline model, a linear regression and a decision tree (13 automatic marks, Weeks 8 and 9)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Question 1.** How much is the lagging reactive power the plant draws in a 15-minute reading, in kVarh, for months the model has never seen?\\n\", \"\\n\", \"`fit_predict_regression(training_records, feature_records)` trains (fits) three models on the training records and predicts for the feature records. Use the columns in `FEATURES` (`Usage_kWh`, `Leading_Current_Reactive_Power_kVarh`, `NSM`, `weekend`), in that order. The feature records have **no target**.\\n\", \"- every model is a pipeline that first does **imputation** of missing values with the **median of the training rows** (`SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\")`), then fits;\\n\", \"- `\\\"baseline\\\"`: the baseline model, which always predicts the mean training target (`DummyRegressor`);\\n\", \"- `\\\"linear\\\"`: multiple linear regression, `LinearRegression`;\\n\", \"- `\\\"tree\\\"`: a decision tree, `DecisionTreeRegressor(max_depth=4, random_state=17)`. The `max_depth` is a **hyperparameter**: a setting you choose before training.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Return a dictionary with three lists of predictions, in the order of the feature records. The baseline model must use the **training** rows only: a baseline built with validation or test targets has **leaked**.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"`regression_errors(actual, predicted)` returns `{\\\"mae\\\": ..., \\\"rmse\\\": ...}` over the same pairs, not rounded. A **residual** is actual minus predicted. MAE (mean absolute error) is the mean of the absolute residuals. RMSE (root mean squared error) is the square root of the mean of the squared residuals.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**No leakage (2 marks).** The marking computer gives your function extra feature rows with extreme values and checks that the predictions for the other rows do not change (the imputer learned its medians from the training rows only), and it shifts the training targets to check that the baseline predicts the mean of the training targets it was given.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Charts (2 marks).** One mark for Chart 1 (Task 1) and one for Chart 2 (below): each needs a title, both axis names, the unit on the y axis, and the right data.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-regression\"]}, \"source\": [\"def fit_predict_regression(training_records, feature_records):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return {\\\"baseline\\\": [...], \\\"linear\\\": [...], \\\"tree\\\": [...]}.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    # TODO 1: table = pd.DataFrame(training_records); new = pd.DataFrame(feature_records)\\n\", \"    # TODO 2: X_train = table[FEATURES]; y_train = table[TARGET]; X_new = new[FEATURES].\\n\", \"    # TODO 3: a dictionary of three pipelines. Each is make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\"), <the model>).\\n\", \"    # TODO 4: loop over the dictionary: fit on (X_train, y_train), predict X_new, and store the predictions as a list of floats under the model's name.\\n\", \"    return None\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-regression\"]}, \"source\": [\"def regression_errors(actual, predicted):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return {\\\"mae\\\": ..., \\\"rmse\\\": ...}, not rounded.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    # TODO: use numpy: residuals = np.array(actual) - np.array(predicted); MAE uses np.abs and the mean; RMSE uses squares, the mean, then np.sqrt.\\n\", \"    return None\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-regression\"]}, \"source\": [\"# Evaluate the three models on the validation set (September and October). Keep the names: validation_predictions, validation_mae and validation_rmse.\\n\", \"validation_features = None    # TODO: the validation rows without the TARGET key\\n\", \"validation_actual = None      # TODO: the list of validation targets\\n\", \"validation_predictions = None # TODO: fit_predict_regression(train_rows, validation_features)\\n\", \"validation_mae = None         # TODO: a dictionary {\\\"baseline\\\": ..., \\\"linear\\\": ..., \\\"tree\\\": ...} of MAE values, from regression_errors\\n\", \"validation_rmse = None        # TODO: the same with RMSE\\n\", \"print(\\\"Validation MAE  (\\\" + TARGET_UNIT + \\\"):\\\", validation_mae)\\n\", \"print(\\\"Validation RMSE (\\\" + TARGET_UNIT + \\\"):\\\", validation_rmse)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"figure, axes = plt.subplots()\\n\", \"# TODO: a bar chart of the three MAE values, with a title, a y label that includes the unit, and the model names on the x axis.\\n\", \"chart_2 = figure\\n\", \"plt.show()\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Self-study note: logistic regression, the confusion matrix, precision and recall\\n\", \"**Logistic regression** gives each row a score (a weighted sum of the features plus a constant) and passes it through the **sigmoid** function, 1 / (1 + e^(-score)). The result is between 0 and 1: the **probability** of a warning. `predict_proba(X)[:, 1]` gives that probability for each row.\\n\", \"The **decision threshold** turns a probability into a yes or no. In this project it is fixed at 0.5: a warning when the probability is 0.5 or more.\\n\", \"The **confusion matrix** counts four kinds of answer: **TP** (a warning that was right), **FP** (a false alarm), **FN** (a missed warning) and **TN** (no warning, rightly). `confusion_matrix(actual, predicted, labels=[0, 1]).ravel()` gives them in the order tn, fp, fn, tp.\\n\", \"**Precision** = TP / (TP + FP): of the warnings raised, the share that were right. **Recall** = TP / (TP + FN): of the real high readings, the share we caught. If a denominator is 0, use 0 (`zero_division=0`).\\n\", \"Slides: Class 2, \\\"Logistic regression passes a linear score through the sigmoid (logistic) function, which stays between 0 and 1\\\", \\\"Logistic regression in three lines: score, probability, prediction\\\", \\\"The confusion matrix counts true and false positives and negatives\\\", \\\"Accuracy and precision are worked out from the four counts TP, FP, FN and TN\\\" and \\\"Recall (sensitivity) and F1: how many actual positives we find, and one blended score\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 4: Question 2: which readings should raise a warning? (3 automatic marks, Weeks 9 and 10)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"**Question 2.** A reading is a **warning** (1, the positive class) when its `Lagging_Current_Reactive.Power_kVarh` is at or above `LIMIT` (40 kVarh) and **no warning** (0, the negative class) below it. A high lagging reactive power is when the plant is most likely to pay a power-factor penalty. The limit is an invented teaching number. Which readings should raise a warning?\\n\", \"\\n\", \"`warning_probabilities(training_records, feature_records, limit)` returns the probability of a warning, for each feature record, from a pipeline of `SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\")`, `StandardScaler()` and `LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)` (binary classification), fitted on the training records only. The label is `1` when the training target is `>= limit`. Use the columns in `FEATURES`. (1 mark)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"`classification_scores(actual, probability)` predicts a warning when the probability is at or above `THRESHOLD` (0.5, the **decision threshold**, the same for the whole project), then returns a dictionary with the confusion matrix counts `tp`, `fp`, `fn`, `tn` (true positives, false positives, false negatives, true negatives), `precision` and `recall`. When a ratio has a zero denominator, use 0. (2 marks)\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-warning\"]}, \"source\": [\"def warning_probabilities(training_records, feature_records, limit):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return a list of warning probabilities, one per feature record.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    # TODO 1: tables as before; y_train = (training target >= limit) turned into 0 and 1 (astype(int)).\\n\", \"    # TODO 2: model = make_pipeline(SimpleImputer(strategy=\\\"median\\\"), StandardScaler(), LogisticRegression(max_iter=1000)); fit it on table[FEATURES].\\n\", \"    # TODO 3: predict_proba(new[FEATURES]) gives two columns: column 1 is the probability of a warning. Return it as a list of floats.\\n\", \"    return None\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-warning\"]}, \"source\": [\"def classification_scores(actual, probability):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Return tp, fp, fn, tn, precision and recall, with a warning when the probability is at or above THRESHOLD.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    # TODO 1: predicted = 1 where the probability is at or above THRESHOLD, else 0 (a list comprehension).\\n\", \"    # TODO 2: tn, fp, fn, tp = confusion_matrix(actual, predicted, labels=[0, 1]).ravel()   (see Class 2)\\n\", \"    # TODO 3: precision_score and recall_score with zero_division=0. Turn numpy numbers into int() and float() before returning.\\n\", \"    return None\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-warning\"]}, \"source\": [\"warning_actual = None        # TODO: 1 or 0 for each validation row: is the target at or above LIMIT?\\n\", \"warning_probability = None   # TODO: warning_probabilities(train_rows, validation_features, LIMIT)\\n\", \"warning_scores = None        # TODO: classification_scores(warning_actual, warning_probability)\\n\", \"print(\\\"Warnings on the validation set:\\\", warning_scores)\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 5: Choose one model with the supplied rule (supplied rule; no marks of its own)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The model-choice rule is supplied (the cell below says Do not edit this cell). Run it, then use it. The rule: keep the models whose validation MAE is at most `MAX_MAE` **and** whose prediction time is at most `MAX_MS` (both limits included); among those, choose the smallest validation MAE; break a tie by the shorter time, then by the name. If no model qualifies, the rule returns `None`. Never use the test set to choose.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"The client for this project is the plant's energy manager. The client accepts a typical prediction error of at most `MAX_MAE` (6 kVarh) and a prediction time of at most `MAX_MS` (50 ms). Both numbers are invented for teaching.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"Your part: build `candidate_table`, one dictionary per model with `name`, `validation_mae` (your own Task 3 value) and `latency_ms` (from `LATENCY_MS`), and call `choose_model(candidate_table, MAX_MAE, MAX_MS)`. This task has no marks of its own: your chosen model counts in the results mark and in Part B.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-support\"], \"cellView\": \"form\"}, \"source\": [\"#@title The model-choice rule - Do not edit this cell\\n\", \"# IUEA-CHOOSE-CELL  (supplied: do not edit)\\n\", \"def choose_model(candidates, max_mae, max_ms):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Keep the candidates with validation_mae <= max_mae and latency_ms <= max_ms. Choose the smallest validation_mae;\\n\", \"    break a tie by the smaller latency_ms, then by the name. Return the chosen name, or None if nobody qualifies.\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    allowed = [c for c in candidates if c[\\\"validation_mae\\\"] <= max_mae and c[\\\"latency_ms\\\"] <= max_ms]\\n\", \"    if not allowed:\\n\", \"        return None\\n\", \"    return sorted(allowed, key=lambda c: (c[\\\"validation_mae\\\"], c[\\\"latency_ms\\\"], c[\\\"name\\\"]))[0][\\\"name\\\"]\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-answer-choose\"]}, \"source\": [\"candidate_table = []     # TODO: one dictionary per model (baseline, linear, tree): name, validation_mae (from your Task 3 results), latency_ms (from LATENCY_MS)\\n\", \"chosen_model = None      # TODO: choose_model(candidate_table, MAX_MAE, MAX_MS)\\n\", \"print(candidate_table)\\n\", \"print(\\\"Chosen model:\\\", chosen_model)\\n\"], \"execution_count\": null, \"outputs\": []}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"### Self-study note: open the test set once, then refit\\n\", \"The validation set is for choosing; the test set is for **one honest estimate** of the model you chose. If you looked at the test set and then changed your choice, the test set would have helped choose, and its score would be too good to trust.\\n\", \"So: choose the model on the validation set (Task 5), then call `open_test_set` **one time**. It **refits** your functions on all the development months (January to October, more data than training alone) and measures the error on November and December, which nothing has used so far. Whatever the result, you report it.\\n\", \"Slides: Class 3, \\\"We choose max_depth on the validation set and keep the test set sealed\\\" and \\\"Keep this checklist: five questions before you trust a score\\\".\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"markdown\", \"metadata\": {}, \"source\": [\"## Task 6: Open the test set, once (2 automatic marks, Week 10)\\n\", \"\\n\", \"November and December are the **test set**: two months that nothing so far has used. They give **one** honest estimate for the model you chose.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"How it works. The supplied cell below is marked Do not edit this cell. Run it once to define `open_test_set(model_name)`. Then call it **one time**, with the model you chose in Task 5. It cleans the whole file with your `clean_rows`, **refits** your functions on all the development months (January to October, more rows than training alone), evaluates on November and December (warnings at the threshold 0.5), prints the result and writes your choice to a small file. If you call it again, the notebook counts a second opening and you lose part of this task's marks, more if the model is different.\\n\", \"\\n\", \"You must not use November or December to choose a model or anything else, and you must not look at them before this call. Report whatever the result is in your Part B form.\"]}, {\"cell_type\": \"code\", \"metadata\": {\"tags\": [\"iuea-support\"], \"cellView\": \"form\"}, \"source\": [\"#@title Open the test set (run it ONCE, at the very end) - Do not edit this cell\\n\", \"# IUEA-TEST-CELL  (supplied: do not edit)\\n\", \"import json as _json\\n\", \"TEST_OPEN_LOG = []\\n\", \"TEST_OUTCOMES = []\\n\", \"_TEST_FILE = \\\"test_first_opening.json\\\"\\n\", \"\\n\", \"def open_test_set(model_name):\\n\", \"    \\\"\\\"\\\"Evaluate ONE chosen model on the test set (November and December), with the functions you wrote. Warnings use THRESHOLD (0.5).\\\"\\\"\\\"\\n\", \"    if model_name not in (\\\"baseline\\\", \\\"linear\\\", \\\"tree\\\"):\\n\", \"        raise ValueError('model_name must be \\\"baseline\\\", \\\"linear\\\" or \\\"tree\\\"')\\n\", \"    first = _json.load(open(_TEST_FILE)) if os.path.exists(_TEST_FILE) else None\\n\", \"    if first is None:\\n\", \"        _json.dump({\\\"model\\\": model_name}, open(_TEST_FILE, \\\"w\\\"))\\n\", \"